## ansible.builtin.replace Module

Purpose:

The replace module searches the entire file for the given regular expression (regexp) and replaces every matching occurrence with the value specified in replace.

Syntax:

- name: Replace text
  ansible.builtin.replace:
    path: <file_path>
    regexp: <pattern_to_search>
    replace: <replacement_text>

Parameters:

path: Specifies the file in which Ansible should perform the replacement.

Example:

path: /etc/redis/redis.conf

------------------------------------------------------------

regexp: Specifies the text or regular expression to search for inside the file.

Example:

regexp: 127.0.0.1
or
regexp: Megham

------------------------------------------------------------

replace: Specifies the new text that should replace every matching occurrence.

Example:

replace: 0.0.0.0
or
replace: muddule-muddulu

------------------------------------------------------------

How it works?

Step 1: Open the specified file.
Step 2: Search the entire file using the regexp.
Step 3: Replace every matching occurrence with the replace value.
Step 4: Save the file.

Example:

Input:

Megham
Megham is my wife

Playbook:

replace:
  regexp: Megham
  replace: muddule-muddulu

Output:

muddule-muddulu
muddule-muddulu is my wife

Observation:

- Searches the entire file.
- Replaces every matching occurrence.
- Replaces only the matched text.
- Does NOT replace the complete line.


Real Example:

- name: Replace bind IP
  ansible.builtin.replace:
    path: /etc/redis/redis.conf
    regexp: 127.0.0.1
    replace: 0.0.0.0

Purpose: Replace the default localhost IP with 0.0.0.0 so Redis listens on all available network interfaces.

============================================================

## ansible.builtin.lineinfile Module

Purpose:

The lineinfile module searches for a matching line using regexp and replaces the entire line with the value specified in line.

Syntax:

- name: Replace line
  ansible.builtin.lineinfile:
    path: <file_path>
    regexp: <pattern_to_search>
    line: <new_line>

Parameters:

path: Specifies the file in which Ansible should search.

Example:

path: /etc/redis/redis.conf

------------------------------------------------------------

regexp: Specifies the text or regular expression used to identify the matching line.

Example:

regexp: protected-mode
or
regexp: Megham

------------------------------------------------------------

line: Specifies the complete line that should replace the matching line.

Example:

line: "protected-mode no"
or
line: muddule-muddulu

------------------------------------------------------------

How it works?

Step 1: Open the specified file.
Step 2: Search for the line matching the regexp.
Step 3: Replace the entire matching line with the line value.
Step 4: Save the file.


Example:

Input:

Megham
Megham is my wife

Playbook:

lineinfile:
  regexp: Megham
  line: muddule-muddulu

Output:

Megham
muddule-muddulu

Observation:

- Searches for matching lines.
- Replaces the entire matching line.
- If multiple lines match, lineinfile replaces the last matching line.
- Does NOT replace every occurrence of the matched word.


Real Example:

- name: Disable protected mode
  ansible.builtin.lineinfile:
    path: /etc/redis/redis.conf
    regexp: protected-mode
    line: "protected-mode no"

Purpose: Find the line containing protected-mode and replace the entire line with protected-mode no.

============================================================

## Understanding regexp

Purpose:

The responsibility of regexp is only to find the matching pattern but What happens after finding the pattern is depends on the module.

replace module
↓
Replaces every matching occurrence.

lineinfile module
↓
Replaces the matching configuration line.

