#)ROLES:
--------

Definition:

*)An Ansible role is a way to keep everything required in a standard directory structure for organizing playbooks, which makes the automation easier to maintain and reuse. 
  Instead of maintaining all services in one large playbook, we can maintain each service as an individual role.
  
*)A role can contain directories such as tasks, files, vars, defaults, handlers, and templates. 
  Ansible automatically looks for main.yml, which acts as the default entry point in directories such as tasks, vars, defaults, handlers, and meta.
  The files and templates directories contain supporting files, so they don't require a main.yml because their files are referenced by tasks when required.


Ansible introduced Roles to improve the maintainability of automation. Instead of keeping all services in a single playbook, 
each service can be maintained as a separate role. This makes it easier to locate and modify a specific service without affecting others. 
If an issue occurs in one service, we can directly work on its role instead of searching through a large playbook. 
In a team environment, multiple DevOps engineers can work on different roles simultaneously, which reduces Git merge conflicts and minimizing 
the risk of deployment failures caused by accidental changes to unrelated services.


Without Roles:

❌ Huge playbook
❌ Difficult to navigate
❌ Difficult to maintain
❌ Risk of accidental modifications
❌ Git merge conflicts when multiple engineers work

With Roles:

✅ Each service is independent.
✅ Easy maintenance.
✅ Better readability.
✅ Reusable.
✅ Multiple engineers can work simultaneously.


Role Structure:
---------------

A role can contain directories such as:

1)tasks/
2)files/
3)handlers/
4)templates/
5)vars/
6)defaults/
7)meta/



1.Tasks
--------

Definition:

The tasks directory contains the actual automation steps (tasks that we normally write in a playbook) that need to be performed for that particular role. 
These tasks are written in tasks/main.yml, which Ansible uses as the default entry point when the role is executed.

Playbook lo tasks ela rastamo, role lo ave tasks → tasks/main.yml lo rastam.

Example:

frontend/
└── tasks/
    └── main.yml
	
main.yaml contains tasks


- name: Install nginx
  ansible.builtin.dnf:
    name: nginx
    state: present

- name: Start nginx
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: nginx
    state: started
	
	
2.files
--------

Definition:

The files directory stores static files required for the role. These files are not automatically executed or loaded by Ansible. 
They are used by a task whenever required, for example, to copy a file to the managed node. This files folder stores ready-made files.

Example:


frontend/
├── tasks/
│   └── main.yml
└── files/
    └── nginx.conf
	
Here nginx.conf is a ready-made file and Ansible just uses/copies it when the task asks for it.

- name: Copy nginx configuration
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    src: nginx.conf
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
	
	
3. Handlers
-----------

Definition:

*)A handler is a special task that runs only when a task with a 'notify' parameter reports a change.
  In a role, handler tasks are written in handlers/main.yml.
  
*)Handlers are used for actions that should be performed only when another task makes an actual change. 
  Restarting or reloading a service after a configuration change is one of the most common use cases.

*) The name specified in 'notify' should match the handler task 'name'.

Tip line: Handler tasks are executed only when another task makes a change and notifies the handler.
---------


Important Points:
-----------------

*) notify does not execute the handler immediately. It notifies and queues the handler to run later.

*) If the task does not make any change, the handler is not notified.

*) If multiple tasks notify the same handler, the handler normally executes only once.

*) A handler is also an Ansible task. The difference is that a normal task executes in the
   normal task flow, whereas a handler executes only when it is notified by a changed task.

Example:

tasks/main.yml

- name: Copy nginx configuration
  ansible.builtin.copy:
    src: nginx.conf
    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
  notify: Restart nginx


handlers/main.yml

- name: Restart nginx
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: nginx
    state: restarted



## Handler Flow:
----------------

First, the normal task executes.
↓
Did the task make a change?
↓
If YES
↓
Notify the handler.
↓
Handler is queued.
↓
Remaining tasks continue to execute.
↓
At the end of the play, the handler executes. ✅



##)vars/:
---------

*)vars/ directory stores the variables that are required for that particular role, so the tasks in that role can use those values instead of hard-coding them.

*)Instead of hard-coding the same value in multiple tasks, we can store it in a variable and manage it from one place. 
  If the value changes, we only need to update the variable.
  
vars/
└── main.yml


Example:
--------
Suppose our role has multiple tasks that need the same package name:

frontend/
├── vars/
│   └── main.yml
└── tasks/
    └── main.yml


vars/main.yml:

package_name: nginx


tasks/main.yml:

- name: Install package
  ansible.builtin.dnf:
    name: "{{ package_name }}"
    state: present

- name: Start service
  ansible.builtin.service:
    name: "{{ package_name }}"
    state: started


Here, both tasks use the same variable:

package_name → nginx

For example, In future if the package/service name changes then we only need to update it in vars/main.yml instead of changing it in every task.


##)defaults/
------------

*)defaults/ directory is also used to define variables, but it stores the default values for variables used by a role. 
  These are default values. If we provide a different value, the provided value will be used instead of the default value. ✅

*)understanding liner: use this value unless someone provides a different value.



Example:

frontend/
├── defaults/
│   └── main.yml
└── tasks/
    └── main.yml


defaults/main.yml:

http_port: 80


tasks/main.yml:

- name: Configure application
  ansible.builtin.template:
    src: app.conf.j2
    dest: /etc/app.conf

app.conf.j2 → this could contain default value


Normally: http_port → 80

If we provide a different value: http_port → 8080

Then: http_port becomes → 8080



##)Difference between vars/ & defaults/ :
-----------------------------------------

*) Both vars/ and defaults/ are used to define variables for a role, but they have different variable precedence.

*) vars/main.yml has higher precedence than defaults/main.yml. So, if the same variable is defined in both vars/main.yml and defaults/main.yml,
   the value from vars/main.yml will be used as this has higher precedence .


*)vars/
    ↓
  Variables with higher precedence


*)defaults/
    ↓
  Default values with lower precedence
    ↓
  Easily overridden


Example:

frontend/
├── vars/
│   └── main.yml
└── defaults/
    └── main.yml


vars/main.yml:

http_port: 8080


defaults/main.yml:

http_port: 80


Both files have the same variable name: 'http_port'


Ansible gives higher priority to vars/main.yml than defaults/main.yml.

So:

vars/main.yml       → http_port = 8080   ← Higher priority
defaults/main.yml   → http_port = 80     ← Lower priority


Final value used by Ansible: http_port → 8080


#)templates/
-------------

Definition:

*)The templates directory stores template files that contain variables/placeholders.
  Ansible templates are Jinja2-formatted files that contain placeholders/variables instead of hard-coded values.
  Ansible takes the template, gets the actual values from variables, and creates the required file on the managed node.

*)Template files are created with the .j2 extension.


{{ MONGODB_HOST }} → placeholder / variable



Example:

catalogue/
├── vars/
│   └── main.yml
├── templates/
│   └── catalogue.service.j2
└── tasks/
    └── main.yml


vars/main.yml :

MONGODB_HOST: mongodb.roboshop


templates/catalogue.service.j2 :

[Unit]
Description = Catalogue Service

[Service]
User=roboshop
Environment=MONGO=true
Environment=MONGO_URL="mongodb://{{ MONGODB_HOST }}:27017/catalogue"
Environment=TOOL="ANSIBLE"
ExecStart=/bin/node /app/server.js
SyslogIdentifier=catalogue
[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target


Here, {{ MONGODB_HOST }} is a variable/placeholder in the template.
The actual value is provided through the role's variables.

The template module processes the template and replaces {{ MONGODB_HOST }} with its
actual value before placing the final file on the managed node.


tasks/main.yml:

- name: Create catalogue service
  ansible.builtin.template:
    src: catalogue.service.j2
    dest: /etc/systemd/system/catalogue.service


Flow:

vars/main.yml
      ↓
MONGODB_HOST: mongodb.roboshop
      ↓
templates/catalogue.service.j2
      ↓
{{ MONGODB_HOST }} is replaced with the actual value
      ↓
template module
      ↓
Final configuration file
      ↓
Managed node


Difference between files/ and templates/:
------------------------------------------

files/
→ Stores static/ready-made files.
→ The copy module copies the file as it is.
→ Variables inside the file are not replaced.

templates/
→ Stores template files containing variables/placeholders.
→ The template module processes the file.
→ Variables are replaced with their actual values.
→ The final file is placed on the managed node.


Simple memory:

files/     → Copy the file as it is.
templates/ → Generate the file using variables.


#)meta/
-------

Definition:

The meta directory contains information about a role. It is mainly used to define the role dependencies.
We define the roles that need to be executed before the current role in meta/main.yml.


Example:

catalogue/
└── meta/
    └── main.yml


meta/main.yml:

dependencies:
- common


Here, the catalogue role depends on the common role.

So, when the catalogue role is executed, Ansible will execute the common role first
and then execute the catalogue role.


